<BaseLayout
  title="What's the Moon Worth? | Population Mars"
  description="NASA agreed to buy lunar soil for $25,001 in 2020 and has received none. What the cislunar economy is paying today, what the forecasts count, who is betting, and who gets to write the rules."
  ogImage="/images/blog/moon-worth-hero.jpg"
  showDonationCTA={false}
  className="moon-worth-page"
>
  <article class="article-shell">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <img
        src="/images/blog/moon-worth-hero.jpg"
        alt="The Moon, half lit from the left with its cratered south polar region tilted toward the viewer, and a small Earth in the distance"
        width="2488"
        height="1384"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <div class="title-card">
        <h1>What&rsquo;s the Moon Worth?</h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="article-grid">
      <aside class="contents" aria-label="Article sections">
        <p>Contents</p>
        <ol>
          {sections.map(([id, label]) => (
            <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </aside>

      <div class="article-copy">
<p class="status"><em>Status as of 2 November 2026. Several of the missions and decisions below are scheduled for the next six months.</em></p>
        <div class="opening">
<p>On 3 December 2020, NASA agreed to buy small samples of lunar soil from four companies. Lunar Outpost bid $1. ispace&rsquo;s Japanese and European subsidiaries bid $5,000 each. Masten Space Systems bid $15,000. The total was $25,001, and <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-selects-companies-to-collect-lunar-resources-for-artemis-demonstrations">the point was legal rather than commercial</a>: a government paying a private company for material collected on the Moon, with title transferring on the surface, under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty.</p>
<p>Six years later none of the four has delivered. Masten filed for bankruptcy in July 2022 and Astrobotic bought its assets for $4.5 million. ispace&rsquo;s first lander crashed in April 2023, and its second, carrying the European subsidiary&rsquo;s rover, hit the surface on 6 June 2025 after <a href="https://2022.ispace-inc.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/20250624-M2-Tech-Cause-Analysis-Press_Release.pdf">its laser rangefinder fed back measurements too late</a>. Lunar Outpost&rsquo;s rover flew on Intuitive Machines&rsquo; Athena lander in March 2025, which came down on its side; the rover never left the deck. Lunar Outpost did collect <a href="https://www.lunaroutpost.com/post/lunar-outpost-becomes-first-company-to-receive-payment-for-space-resources">a milestone payment in 2021</a>, of ten cents.</p>
<p>The most-cited sale in lunar commerce is worth less than a used car, and it has not closed.</p>
        </div>

        <section id="who-is-getting-paid">
          <h2>1. Who is actually getting paid</h2>
<p>Almost all the money moving toward the Moon today comes from governments, mostly NASA. The largest awards are for vehicles and services NASA wants for its own astronauts.</p>

          <figure class="fig bars-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-awards-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 1</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-awards-title">The largest disclosed lunar awards, and the smallest</p>
            <p class="fig-legend" aria-hidden="true"><span class="key solid"></span>Awarded value <span class="key outline"></span>Contract ceiling (maximum, not money spent) <span class="key hi"></span>Resources</p>
            <ul class="bars">
              {awardGroups.map((group) => (
                <>
                  <li class="bar-group"><span class="bar-group-name">{group.name}</span></li>
                  {group.rows.map((row) => (
                    <li class={`bar-row${group.hi ? ' hi' : ''}${row.ceiling ? ' ceiling' : ''}`} title={`${row.name}: ${row.label}`}>
                      <span class="bar-name">{row.name}</span>
                      <span class="bar-track"><span class="bar" style={`--w:${pct(row.value)}`}></span><span class="bar-val">{row.label}</span></span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </>
              ))}
            </ul>
            <figcaption>U.S. dollars, nominal, linear scale. CLPS rows sum the task orders each company has been awarded since 2019; not all of that has been paid, and the Blue Origin row includes the VIPER delivery option. Intuitive Machines&rsquo; IM-1 to IM-3 values are from NASA&rsquo;s original award announcements. Sources: NASA, company releases, <a href="https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/nasa-awards-initial-moon-base-rover-and-lander-contracts/">SpacePolicyOnline</a>, <a href="https://www.satellitetoday.com/space-economy/2026/06/30/astrobotic-firefly-aerospace-and-intuitive-machines-win-nasa-lunar-delivery-missions/">Via Satellite</a>, <a href="https://www.interlune.space/press-release/interlune-awarded-6-9-million-contract-from-nasa-for-lunar-resource-development">Interlune</a>.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>SpaceX holds $4.04 billion in Human Landing System awards from 2021 and 2022, and Blue Origin holds a $3.4 billion award from May 2023. Intuitive Machines holds a <a href="https://investors.intuitivemachines.com/news-releases/news-release-details/nasa-awards-intuitive-machines-near-space-network-contract">Near Space Network relay contract</a> with a ceiling of $4.82 billion through 2034, though a ceiling is a maximum and not money spent. Below those sit the Commercial Lunar Payload Services (CLPS) task orders, which pay companies to deliver NASA instruments to the surface. The original CLPS contract had a $2.6 billion ceiling through 2028. On 26 May 2026 NASA picked Astrolab ($219 million) and Lunar Outpost ($220 million) to build lunar terrain vehicles, and on 30 June it handed Astrobotic, Firefly and Intuitive Machines <a href="https://www.satellitetoday.com/space-economy/2026/06/30/astrobotic-firefly-aerospace-and-intuitive-machines-win-nasa-lunar-delivery-missions/">another $590 million</a> in delivery orders between them.</p>
<p>NASA has since reorganized around this spending. On 24 March 2026 it announced a plan to spend <a href="https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/business/2026/03/24/nasa-to-spend-us20-billion-on-moon-base-cancel-orbiting-lunar-station/">about $20 billion over seven years</a> on a base near the south pole and to pause the Gateway station. In February it had already changed Artemis III from a landing into <a href="https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/02/27/nasa-announces-major-overhaul-of-artemis-moon-program/">a crewed docking test in Earth orbit</a>, now targeted for summer 2027, which moves the first landing to Artemis IV in 2028. Artemis II flew four astronauts around the Moon from 1 to 10 April 2026, the first people beyond low Earth orbit since December 1972. The administration&rsquo;s <a href="https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/trump-fy2027-budget-supports-moon-missions-but-cuts-everything-else/">FY2027 request</a> asks for $8.5 billion for Artemis inside a NASA budget cut 23 percent overall.</p>
<p>Other governments buy too. ESA signed an &euro;862 million contract with a Thales Alenia Space-led team in January 2025 for the Argonaut cargo lander, first mission in 2031, and a &euro;123 million first phase of its <a href="https://www.telespazio.com/en/news-and-stories-detail/-/detail/thales-alenia-space-moonlight-telespazio">Moonlight</a> communications system with Telespazio in October 2024. Japan funds ispace through subsidies; most of ispace&rsquo;s project revenue in the year to March 2026 was subsidy income, and the company forecasts a &yen;13 billion net loss for the current year.</p>
<p>What government money has bought so far is a poor landing record.</p>

          <figure class="fig score-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-landings-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 2</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-landings-title">Commercial lunar landing attempts since April 2023</p>
            <ol class="scorecard">
              <li class="fail"><time>Apr 2023</time><b>Hakuto-R M1</b><span class="who">ispace</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-fail" aria-hidden="true"></span>Crashed</span></li>
              <li class="none"><time>Jan 2024</time><b>Peregrine</b><span class="who">Astrobotic, CLPS</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-none" aria-hidden="true"></span>Propellant leak, no landing attempt</span></li>
              <li class="partial"><time>Feb 2024</time><b>IM-1 Odysseus</b><span class="who">Intuitive Machines, CLPS</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-partial" aria-hidden="true"></span>Landed on its side</span></li>
              <li class="ok"><time>Mar 2025</time><b>Blue Ghost M1</b><span class="who">Firefly, CLPS</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-ok" aria-hidden="true"></span>Landed upright, full mission</span></li>
              <li class="partial"><time>Mar 2025</time><b>IM-2 Athena</b><span class="who">Intuitive Machines, CLPS</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-partial" aria-hidden="true"></span>Landed on its side, ended next day</span></li>
              <li class="fail"><time>Jun 2025</time><b>Resilience M2</b><span class="who">ispace</span><span class="outcome"><span class="glyph g-fail" aria-hidden="true"></span>Crashed</span></li>
              <li class="empty"><time>2026</time><b>No attempts</b><span class="who">Griffin, IM-3, Blue Ghost M2 and Blue Moon MK1 slipped</span><span class="outcome">&mdash;</span></li>
            </ol>
            <figcaption>Six attempts, one clean landing. Outcome is shown by symbol and label: filled circle, landed upright; half circle, landed on its side; open circle, no landing attempt; cross, crashed. Sources: <a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/science/private-lunar-lander-successfully-touches-down-on-the-moon-with-a-special-delivery-for-nasa">PBS</a>, <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-receives-some-data-before-intuitive-machines-ends-lunar-mission/">NASA</a>, <a href="https://www.pbs.org/newshour/science/japanese-private-lunar-lander-falls-silent-while-attempting-moon-touchdown">PBS</a>, company statements.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>Six commercial attempts since April 2023 produced one clean landing, Firefly&rsquo;s Blue Ghost Mission 1 on 2 March 2025. Government landers have done better in the same period: India&rsquo;s Chandrayaan-3 in August 2023 and China&rsquo;s Chang&rsquo;e-6 far-side sample return in June 2024. No lander of any kind has reached the surface in 2026. Astrobotic&rsquo;s Griffin, Intuitive Machines&rsquo; IM-3 and Firefly&rsquo;s second Blue Ghost have all slipped toward 2027, a New Glenn booster <a href="https://payloadspace.com/blue-origins-new-glenn-explodes-on-the-pad/">exploded during a static fire</a> on 28 May 2026 and grounded Blue Origin&rsquo;s first lander, and China <a href="https://spacenews.com/china-delays-change-7-lunar-south-pole-landing-mission-launch/">postponed Chang&rsquo;e-7</a> hours before its August launch.</p>
<p>The market research firm NSR, now part of Analysys Mason, put the government and military share of the lunar market it forecast at <a href="https://www.space2connect.esa.int/media/presentations/S2C23_SESSION2.pdf">79 percent</a> in 2023. Our estimate for revenue already earned is higher, above 90 percent, because almost every lander dollar so far has come from NASA, ESA or Japan&rsquo;s subsidy programs. The wider space economy runs the other way: the Space Foundation counted <a href="https://www.exterrajsc.com/p/global-space-economy-grows-12-to">$686 billion in 2025</a>, of which about 79 percent was commercial.</p>
<p class="callout">If you have bought payload space on a lunar lander, we would like to know what you paid per kilogram and what you got back. Send it to <a href="mailto:info@populationmars.com">info@populationmars.com</a>. The public price lists stop at &ldquo;contact us.&rdquo;</p>
        </section>

        <section id="five-forecasts">
          <h2>2. Five forecasts measuring five different things</h2>
<p>Most lunar-economy headlines trace back to a handful of studies. They count different quantities over different periods, so their totals cannot be compared or added.</p>
<div class="table-shell" role="region" aria-label="Lunar market forecasts and what each counts" tabindex="0"><table class="forecast-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Forecast</th><th>Figure</th><th>What it counts</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><a href="https://space-economy.esa.int/article/119/pwcs-lunar-market-assessment-market-trends-and-challenges-in-the-development-of-a-lunar-economy">PwC, 2021</a></td><td>~$170B (&euro;142B)</td><td>Cumulative lunar market, 2020&ndash;2040: transport, data, resource use</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.nsr.com/?p=32458">NSR, 2022</a></td><td>$105B</td><td>Lunar market over the following decade, 250+ missions</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.space2connect.esa.int/media/presentations/S2C23_SESSION2.pdf">NSR / Analysys Mason, 2023</a></td><td>$136.7B</td><td>Cumulative to 2032; 79% of it government or military</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.pwc.fr/en/publications/2026/01/lunar-market-assessment.html">PwC, Jan 2026</a></td><td>$93.9B&ndash;$127.3B</td><td>Lunar revenue by 2050 across mobility, communications, habitation, energy and water; $72.7B&ndash;$88.5B of infrastructure investment counted separately</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/about/press-room/deloitte-report-lunar-economy-could-generate-up-to-566-billion-in-economic-value-through-2050.html">Deloitte, Aug 2026</a></td><td>$343B&ndash;$566B</td><td>Cumulative &ldquo;economic value&rdquo; to 2050, discounted to present value; not revenue</td></tr>
<tr class="context-row"><td><a href="https://www.exterrajsc.com/p/global-space-economy-grows-12-to">Space Foundation</a>, for scale</td><td>$686B</td><td>The whole space economy in one year, 2025</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>NSR raised its decade estimate from $105 billion in 2022 to $136.7 billion in 2023. Since then the tone has cooled. In August 2026 Analysys Mason, which now owns NSR, <a href="https://www.analysysmason.com/research/content/articles/moon-base-plans/">counted 116 planned lunar missions</a> over the next ten years, 64 of them government-led, and wrote that NASA&rsquo;s base plans push the market further into the 2030s and make it more concentrated than earlier forecasts suggested.</p>
<p>Space forecasts have missed before. In 2002 Futron and Zogby <a href="https://www.americaspace.com/wp-content/uploads/space_docs/Space_Tourism_Market_Study_Futron_2002.pdf">projected</a> that suborbital tourism would carry more than 15,000 passengers a year and earn more than $700 million by 2021. In 2021, fewer than twenty passengers flew on suborbital tourist flights.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="ice">
          <h2>3. Ice nobody has touched</h2>
<p>Every resource business plan for the Moon begins with water ice in the permanently shadowed craters near the poles. The evidence that it exists is good. The evidence for how much sits where, and in what form, is thin.</p>
<p>The best direct number is still from 9 October 2009, when NASA&rsquo;s LCROSS mission drove a rocket stage into Cabeus crater and measured about 155 kilograms of water in the plume, <a href="https://www.aaas.org/news/science-lcross-impact-ejects-minerals-and-frozen-water-crater-moon">an estimated 5.6 percent ice by mass</a> at that spot, plus or minus 2.9. Spectra from India&rsquo;s Chandrayaan-1 <a href="https://moon.nasa.gov/news/59/ice-confirmed-at-the-moons-poles/">confirmed exposed surface ice</a> at both poles in 2018. In May 2026 a study of Chandrayaan-2 radar data reported possible subsurface ice in four of nine doubly shadowed craters near the south pole.</p>
<p>The missions that were supposed to measure ice in place keep failing to arrive. NASA canceled the VIPER rover in July 2024 after spending about $450 million, then <a href="https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/viper-might-have-a-new-ride-to-the-moon-on-blue-origin/">revived it in September 2025</a> with a $190 million option for Blue Origin to deliver it, now no earlier than late 2027. The <a href="https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-lunar-trailblazer-moon-mission-ends/">Lunar Trailblazer</a> orbiter lost contact the day after launch in February 2025. PRIME-1, the drill and mass spectrometer on Athena, ended up running sideways on a tipped lander. ESA&rsquo;s PROSPECT drill is booked on Intuitive Machines&rsquo; IM-4, around 2027.</p>
<p>As of November 2026, no one has drilled into the ground at a candidate mining site and measured ice. A Starpath Robotics job listing this year said it plainly: lunar ice accessibility remains unproven.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="helium-3">
          <h2>4. Helium-3, worked through</h2>
<p>Helium-3 is the lunar resource with the most signed paper behind it, and it has nothing to do with fusion power. The buyers want it for dilution refrigerators, which cool quantum computers and other instruments to millikelvin temperatures, and for neutron detectors.</p>
<p>Interlune, founded by former Blue Origin executives, has assembled the agreements. Maybell Quantum <a href="https://www.interlune.space/press-release/interlune-announces-maybell-quantum-as-first-commercial-customer-to-buy-helium-3">agreed in May 2025</a> to buy thousands of liters for delivery between 2029 and 2035. The U.S. Department of Energy&rsquo;s Isotope Program agreed to buy 3 liters by April 2029, which DOE described as its first purchase of a resource from beyond Earth. In September 2025 <a href="https://bluefors.com/press-releases/bluefors-to-source-Helium-3-from-the-moon-with-interlune-to-power-next-phase-of-quantum-industry-growth/">Bluefors</a>, a Finnish maker of dilution refrigerators, agreed to buy up to 10,000 liters a year from 2028 to 2037, reported as worth about $300 million. Interlune says its agreements total nearly $500 million; that figure is the company&rsquo;s own. It raised an $18 million seed round in March 2024 and two $5 million convertible notes since. In May 2026 NASA gave it <a href="https://www.interlune.space/press-release/interlune-awarded-6-9-million-contract-from-nasa-for-lunar-resource-development">$6.9 million</a> for a payload that will heat regolith and measure the gas it releases, on a CLPS lander in 2028. With Vermeer it has built a <a href="https://payloadspace.com/interlune-unveils-prototype-lunar-helium-3-excavator/">full-scale excavator prototype</a> designed to take in 100 tonnes of regolith an hour.</p>
<p>The difficulty is concentration. Apollo samples hold helium-3 at roughly 2 to 26 parts per billion. Here is what the Bluefors volume implies, using our own arithmetic and generous assumptions.</p>

          <figure class="fig he3-fig" aria-labelledby="fig-he3-title">
            <p class="fig-kicker">Figure 3</p>
            <p class="fig-title" id="fig-he3-title">What one Bluefors-sized year of helium-3 takes</p>
            <ol class="chain">
              <li><span class="chain-val">10,000 L</span><span class="chain-what">of helium-3 gas a year, the Bluefors maximum</span></li>
              <li><span class="chain-val">1.35 kg</span><span class="chain-what">the same gas by mass (about 7,430 liters per kilogram at standard conditions)</span></li>
              <li><span class="chain-val">135,000&ndash;560,000 t</span><span class="chain-what">of regolith dug and heated a year, at 10 and 2.4 parts per billion, with every atom recovered</span></li>
              <li><span class="chain-val">1,350&ndash;5,600 h</span><span class="chain-what">of excavator time a year at Interlune&rsquo;s 100 tonnes per hour; a year has 8,760</span></li>
              <li><span class="chain-val">~21 GWh</span><span class="chain-what">to heat the low case to about 700&nbsp;&deg;C with no heat recovered, an average of about 2.4 MW</span></li>
              <li class="chain-end"><span class="chain-val">~$27M</span><span class="chain-what">a year in revenue, at the roughly $20 million per kilogram Interlune cites</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="power-compare" role="img" aria-label="Average heating power of about 2.4 megawatts compared with NASA's 100 kilowatt lunar reactor goal, 24 times larger">
              <p class="pc-title">Average heating power vs. NASA&rsquo;s first lunar reactor goal</p>
              <div class="pc-row"><span class="pc-name">Heating, low case</span><span class="pc-track"><span class="pc-bar hi" style="--w:100%"></span></span><span class="pc-val">~2.4 MW</span></div>
              <div class="pc-row"><span class="pc-name">NASA reactor goal</span><span class="pc-track"><span class="pc-bar" style="--w:4.17%"></span></span><span class="pc-val">0.1 MW</span></div>
            </div>
            <figcaption>Population Mars arithmetic. Assumes regolith heat capacity of about 0.8&nbsp;kJ/kg&middot;K and a temperature rise of about 700&nbsp;K; sorting the soil first, as Interlune plans to test, would reduce the mass heated. Grades from Apollo samples; excavator rate and price from Interlune; reactor goal from NASA&rsquo;s August 2025 directive.</figcaption>
          </figure>

<p>At 10 parts per billion with every atom recovered, 1.35 kilograms means digging and heating about 135,000 tonnes of regolith a year. At 2.4 parts per billion it is about 560,000 tonnes. Laszlo Keszthelyi of the U.S. Geological Survey has estimated that real recovery puts the figure at 100,000 to 1,000,000 tonnes per kilogram, and described the resource as probably unmineable. Heating even the low case to around 700&nbsp;&deg;C, without recovering any of the heat, takes on the order of 21 gigawatt-hours a year, an average of about 2.4 megawatts. NASA&rsquo;s goal for its first lunar reactor is 100 kilowatts.</p>
<p>Interlune&rsquo;s NASA-funded payload includes a sorter, which suggests it plans to heat only part of what it digs, and its 2027 and 2028 payloads are meant to measure grades at real sites before anyone builds a plant. That is the right order to do things in. At the roughly $20 million per kilogram Interlune cites, the Bluefors volume is worth about $27 million a year, which matches the reported size of the deal. The company is also separating helium-3 from terrestrial helium on Earth, which is the more certain business in the near term.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="propellant">
          <h2>5. Propellant, and why Starship cuts both ways</h2>
<p>The older argument for lunar ice is rocket fuel. In 2016 United Launch Alliance <a href="https://www.space.com/33297-satellite-refueling-business-proposal-ula.html">said it would pay</a> $3,000 per kilogram for propellant delivered to low Earth orbit, $1,000 at the Earth-Moon L1 point and $500 on the lunar surface. A 2019 study led by the Colorado School of Mines <a href="https://www.space.com/moon-mining-space-exploration-report.html">estimated</a> that mining 2,450 tonnes of lunar water a year could support about $2.4 billion a year in propellant sales.</p>
<p>Phil Metzger, a planetary scientist at the University of Central Florida, concluded in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.09011">a 2023 paper in Acta Astronautica</a> that lunar propellant can be commercially viable, and that the deciding variable is how much it costs to land the mining hardware. That is where Starship enters on both sides. If launch from Earth becomes very cheap, Earth-launched propellant undercuts lunar propellant in low Earth orbit, and the lunar product only competes near the Moon. If landing hardware on the Moon becomes very cheap, the mine gets cheaper to build. Neither has happened yet. On 23 October, NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman was still describing a Starship ship-to-ship tanking test as a goal for the end of this year. Both SpaceX&rsquo;s lunar lander and any depot business depend on it.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="relays">
          <h2>6. The relay China already has</h2>
<p>Communications and navigation are the one lunar market with a customer that has already signed, because every surface mission needs a way to talk to Earth from the far side and the poles. They are also the area where the gap between plan and hardware is clearest.</p>

