          </figure>

<p>China&rsquo;s Queqiao-2 relay launched on 20 March 2024 and supported the Chang&rsquo;e-6 far-side landing that June. No Western lunar relay has flown. SSTL&rsquo;s Lunar Pathfinder was still in testing in October 2026. Intuitive Machines&rsquo; first relay satellite slipped with IM-3 into the first quarter of 2027.</p>
<p>Intuitive Machines is building around that contract anyway. It closed its purchase of Lanteris, formerly Maxar Space Systems, on 13 January 2026 for <a href="https://washingtontechnology.com/companies/2025/11/intuitive-machines-looks-beyond-moon-800m-acquisition/409330/">about $800 million</a>, then bought the Goonhilly Earth Station in Cornwall. Its backlog rose from $213 million at the end of 2025 to <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/0001844452/000162828026056821/lunr-20260630.htm">$1.76 billion on 30 June 2026</a>, and its 2026 revenue guidance is $900 million to $1 billion. Most of that growth comes from satellite manufacturing for Earth orbit. The most prominent lunar company in the United States is becoming a satellite company that also goes to the Moon.</p>
<p>Data storage on the Moon is a far smaller line. Lonestar Data Holdings flew an 8-terabyte storage payload on Athena in 2025, <a href="https://www.datacenterdynamics.com/en/news/lunar-data-center-firm-lonestar-data-raises-66m-swaps-ceo/">raised $6.6 million</a> in January 2026, and has a <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250402686697/en">preliminary $120 million agreement</a> with Sidus Space for storage spacecraft at Earth-Moon L1 between 2027 and 2030. It is not a firm order.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="tourism">
          <h2>7. Tourism</h2>
<p>dearMoon, Yusaku Maezawa&rsquo;s planned Starship flight around the Moon, was canceled on 1 June 2024. Space Adventures sold a Soyuz circumlunar seat for $100 million in 2005 and raised the price to $150 million in 2011; that flight never happened either. Blue Origin <a href="https://payloadspace.com/blue-pauses-space-tourism-to-focus-on-the-moon/">paused New Shepard</a> on 30 January 2026 to concentrate on its lunar lander. As of November 2026 no private customer has a scheduled flight to the Moon, and no company has published a price.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="who-is-betting">
          <h2>8. Who is betting anyway</h2>
<p>The largest private bets are on transport, and they rest on NASA&rsquo;s lander contracts.</p>
<p>On 8 February 2026 Elon Musk <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/elon-musk-says-spacex-to-prioritize-landing-on-the-moon-instead-of-mars-city/">said SpaceX would prioritize</a> a &ldquo;self-growing city&rdquo; on the Moon, achievable in under ten years, ahead of Mars. Four months later SpaceX listed on Nasdaq, raising $75 billion. Blue Origin&rsquo;s pause of its suborbital tourism business is a bet in the same direction. Firefly <a href="https://www.washingtontechnology.com/companies/2025/08/firefly-captures-868m-ipo-proceeds/407318">went public</a> on 7 August 2025 at $45 a share, raising $868 million, and traded under $20 by December 2025; its backlog stood at about $1.5 billion in June 2026.</p>
<p>The smaller bets are on resources and services: Interlune on helium-3, Starpath Robotics on ice mining with a <a href="https://payloadspace.com/starpath-raises-12m-for-lunar-ice-mining/">$12 million seed round</a>, Blue Origin&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.blueorigin.com/news/blue-alchemist-hits-major-milestone-toward-permanent-sustainable-lunar-infrastructure">Blue Alchemist</a> on making solar cells from regolith with $34.7 million in NASA money, ICON on building with regolith under a $57.2 million NASA contract. Our <a href="/space-economy/#lunar-title">space economy directory</a> lists the companies working on lunar delivery and resources, with links to their recent funding.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="south-pole">
          <h2>9. Who owns the south pole</h2>
<p>Article II of the <a href="https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/outerspacetreaty.html">Outer Space Treaty</a> says the Moon &ldquo;is not subject to national appropriation by claim of sovereignty, by means of use or occupation, or by any other means.&rdquo; More than 115 states are parties. Article I calls the exploration and use of space &ldquo;the province of all mankind.&rdquo; Article IX requires states to act with &ldquo;due regard&rdquo; to the interests of others and to consult before activities that could cause &ldquo;potentially harmful interference.&rdquo; The treaty is silent on whether a company can own what it digs up.</p>
<p>Four countries have answered that question in national law. The U.S. Commercial Space Launch Competitiveness Act of 2015 says American citizens are entitled to space resources they obtain, &ldquo;including to possess, own, transport, use, and sell&rdquo; them. Luxembourg followed in 2017, the UAE in 2019 and Japan in 2021. The ispace sale to NASA was <a href="https://www.ispace-inc.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/EN_ispace_Space-Resources-Act_FINAL.pdf">licensed under Japan&rsquo;s law</a> and Luxembourg&rsquo;s.</p>
<p>The 1979 <a href="https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/treaties/moon-agreement.html">Moon Agreement</a> took the other position. Article 11 calls the Moon&rsquo;s resources &ldquo;the common heritage of mankind&rdquo; and commits parties to set up an international regime to share the benefits once mining becomes feasible. The United States, the Soviet Union and China never joined. It has 17 parties. Saudi Arabia&rsquo;s <a href="https://anzsilperspective.com/saudi-arabias-withdrawal-from-the-moon-agreement/">withdrawal took effect</a> on 5 January 2024, the first withdrawal from any of the UN space treaties. By our count, 12 of the remaining 17 parties have also signed the Artemis Accords, which take the opposite view of resources.</p>

<div class="table-shell" role="region" aria-label="Legal instruments that bear on lunar resources" tabindex="0"><table class="law-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Year</th><th>Who is in</th><th>On resources</th><th>Binding?</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Outer Space Treaty</td><td>1967</td><td>More than 115 states</td><td>No national appropriation (Art. II); silent on extracted resources</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Liability Convention</td><td>1972</td><td>About 100 states</td><td>Liability for damage on the Moon only where fault is shown (Art. III)</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moon Agreement</td><td>1979</td><td>17 states</td><td>&ldquo;Common heritage of mankind&rdquo;; resources in place cannot be property; future international regime (Art. 11)</td><td>Yes, for parties</td></tr>
<tr><td>U.S. CSLCA, Title IV</td><td>2015</td><td>United States</td><td>Citizens may own and sell resources they obtain</td><td>National law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Luxembourg, UAE, Japan laws</td><td>2017&ndash;2021</td><td>Three states</td><td>Licensed operators can own what they extract</td><td>National law</td></tr>
<tr><td>Artemis Accords</td><td>2020</td><td>78 signatories</td><td>Extraction &ldquo;does not inherently constitute national appropriation&rdquo; (Sec. 10); safety zones (Sec. 11)</td><td>No, political</td></tr>
<tr><td>ILRS agreements</td><td>2021</td><td>About 13 states</td><td>No published position on resource ownership</td><td>No, memoranda</td></tr>
<tr><td>COPUOS draft principles</td><td>2027 target</td><td>All COPUOS members, by consensus</td><td>Extraction is not by itself appropriation; benefit-sharing &ldquo;considered&rdquo;</td><td>No</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<p>The practical contest is narrower than any treaty. Ice sits in permanently shadowed craters, and the places that get sunlight almost continuously, useful for power and for staying warm, sit on crater rims nearby. Martin Elvis, Tony Milligan and Alanna Krolikowski estimated in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.01989">a 2016 paper in Space Policy</a> that the most illuminated peaks near the south pole total about one square kilometer. Using one, they argued, requires keeping others from disturbing it, which comes close to appropriation in practice. NASA&rsquo;s Artemis candidate landing regions all lie within six degrees of the south pole. Chang&rsquo;e-7&rsquo;s preferred target is <a href="https://www.planetary.org/space-missions/change-7">the rim of Shackleton crater</a>.</p>
<p>Section 11 of the <a href="https://www3.nasa.gov/specials/artemis-accords/img/Artemis-Accords-signed-13Oct2020.pdf">Artemis Accords</a> lets signatories declare &ldquo;safety zones&rdquo; around their operations, which are supposed to be temporary, scaled to the activity and publicly notified. Critics say a zone around a power plant on a sunlit ridge can become a claim in all but name. The U.S. government has used the same argument about others. In August 2025 Acting Administrator Sean Duffy directed NASA to field a lunar reactor of at least 100 kilowatts by about 2030. His directive warned that the first country to deploy one <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/nasa-boosts-plans-for-nuclear-reactor-on-the-moon/">&ldquo;could declare a keep-out zone which would significantly inhibit the United States.&rdquo;</a> Russia and China signed a memorandum on a lunar power plant in May 2025, and in December Roscosmos <a href="https://wnn.world-nuclear.org/articles/russia-targeting-lunar-power-station-by-2036">contracted NPO Lavochkin</a> to build one by 2036.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="two-clubs">
          <h2>10. Two clubs and a working group</h2>

