          </figure>

<p>The Artemis Accords are a set of non-binding political commitments that NASA and the State Department opened with eight signatories on 13 October 2020. Section 10 says that extracting space resources &ldquo;does not inherently constitute national appropriation.&rdquo; Vietnam became the 78th signatory on 28 October 2026. Nineteen countries signed this year, more than in any previous year, and many of them, including C&ocirc;te d&rsquo;Ivoire, Morocco, Mauritius, Uzbekistan and Vietnam, are not spacefaring powers.</p>
<p>China and Russia signed the agreement for the International Lunar Research Station in March 2021, with a basic station near the south pole targeted for 2035. China has set <a href="https://spacenews.com/china-wants-50-countries-involved-in-its-ilrs-moon-base/">a goal of 50 countries</a> and international organizations within ten years. Independent counts put national members at about 13; CNSA says 17 countries and organizations. Thailand, Senegal and Serbia have joined both.</p>
<p>The multilateral track runs through Vienna. A <a href="https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/copuos/lsc/space-resources/index.html">working group</a> of the UN Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space (COPUOS), chaired by the Australian lawyer Steven Freeland, has been drafting an initial set of recommended principles for space resource activities. Drafts tabled in April 2026 say that extraction consistent with the Outer Space Treaty does not by itself amount to appropriation, keep resources &ldquo;in place&rdquo; as part of outer space, and treat benefit-sharing as something to be &ldquo;considered&rdquo; as appropriate. Iran and other delegations have pressed for language on unequal access and equitable shares. The United States <a href="https://vienna.usmission.gov/u-s-statement-agenda-item-9-65th-session-of-the-copuos-lsc-april-2026/">told the subcommittee</a> in April that debating how to share benefits from resources not yet extracted is counterproductive. Intersessional meetings started on 24 September, and the group aims for consensus at the Legal Subcommittee in April 2027. Whatever comes out will be non-binding. <a href="https://opiniojuris.org/2026/09/25/space-resources-and-the-anatomy-of-consensus-at-copuos/">Opinio Juris</a> has a readable account of how the drafts reached their current wording.</p>
<p>For a country without a launch program, the bargain on offer has changed since 1979. The Moon Agreement promised a share of revenue from an international regime. The Accords offer access to missions and partners, in exchange for accepting that whoever extracts a resource can own it. Most of the countries signing this year have taken that bargain. At a meeting of Accords signatories in Antalya in October, ISRO&rsquo;s chairman urged that discussions under the Accords move into COPUOS, where everyone is in the room.</p>

