        <section id="lunar-time">
          <h2>11. The first rule will be a clock</h2>
<p>A clock on the Moon runs about 58.7 microseconds a day faster than one on Earth, because gravity there is weaker. That is enough to matter for navigation, where a microsecond of timing error is about 300 meters of position error. In April 2024 the White House Office of Science and Technology Policy <a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/science/space/white-house-nasa-create-standard-time-moon-rcna146373">told NASA to deliver a strategy</a> for Coordinated Lunar Time by 31 December 2026. The International Astronomical Union <a href="https://iau.org/static/resolutions/IAU2024_Resol2_English.pdf">defined a lunar coordinate time</a> in August 2024, and in October 2026 the General Conference on Weights and Measures <a href="https://www.bipm.org/en/cgpm-2026/resolution-6">adopted a resolution</a> on an international lunar reference time scale traceable to UTC. NASA had not published its strategy by the start of November.</p>
<p>Of everything discussed here, lunar time is the one standard likely to be agreed by every spacefaring country before anyone mines anything. Every lander and relay will need it, Chinese ones included. Ownership of a sunlit ridge will be settled later, and probably by whoever is standing on it.</p>
        </section>

